+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is it better to sell your house first, then buy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Selling first is better when you need the money from your sale to buy the next home. The cost is time between homes: about 2 months for a typical Horry County owner who sold first. A rent-back in the home you sold can shorten that gap."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you close on both houses on the same day?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. About 1 in 9 Horry County owners who sold one home and bought another here had both deeds recorded on the same day. Both closing dates are set for that day, and the purchase offer depends on the sale."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a kick-out clause in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"South Carolina's forms do not use that name. The closest thing is a notice, form SCR505, under the sale-contingency addendum, form SCR504. A seller can send it when an offer comes in that does not depend on another sale. The SC REALTORS' legal staff say agents sometimes call the addendum a right of first refusal or a knockout clause."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long can a seller rent back after closing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As long as the buyer agrees in writing. A buyer who will live in the home and has a loan sold to Fannie Mae must move in within 60 days."}}]}</script>
